  <section>
    <h2 id="venues">Two Venues: Which One Should You Visit?</h2>
    <p>For the first time, bauma CHINA uses two exhibition centers in Shanghai's Pudong district.</p>
    <h3>Venue 1: Shanghai New International Expo Centre (SNIEC)</h3>
    <p><strong>November 24–27, 2026</strong></p>
    <p>Address: 2345 Longyang Road, Pudong, Shanghai</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="SNIEC opening hours (Shanghai time)" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Opening hours</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">November 24–26</th><td>9:00 AM–5:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">November 27</th><td>9:00 AM–3:00 PM</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>SNIEC is the main venue for established construction machinery sectors, including heavy equipment, mining machinery, construction vehicles, components and related technologies.</p>
    <p>It is conveniently reached by Metro Line 7 at Huamu Road Station.</p>
    <h3>Venue 2: Shanghai World Expo Exhibition &amp; Convention Center (SWEECC)</h3>
    <p><strong>November 23–26, 2026</strong></p>
    <p>Address: 1099 Guozhan Road, Pudong, Shanghai</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="SWEECC opening hours (Shanghai time)" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Opening hours</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">November 23–25</th><td>9:00 AM–5:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">November 26</th><td>9:00 AM–3:00 PM</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>SWEECC adds space for emerging technologies, industry conferences, innovation-focused exhibits and demonstrations.</p>
    <p>It is accessible via China Art Museum Station on Metro Line 8.</p>
    <h3>Which venue should you choose?</h3>
    <p>If you're attending to meet particular manufacturers or suppliers, <strong>check the exhibitor directory before deciding which day and venue to visit</strong>.</p>
    <p>The two venues are approximately 8 km apart. You can visit both, but changing venues takes time.</p>
    <p><a href="https://community.b-china.cn/en/exhibitions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check the official bauma CHINA exhibitor directory</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="visa">Can You Attend bauma CHINA Without a Chinese Visa?</h2>
    <p>Possibly. It depends on your passport and travel plans.</p>
    <p>Some visitors can enter China under a visa-exemption arrangement. Others may qualify for China's <strong>240-hour visa-free transit policy</strong>, which permits certain short business activities during an eligible transit stay.</p>
    <p>For example, a route such as:</p>
    <p><strong>United States → Shanghai → Japan</strong></p>
    <p>can meet the transit route requirement because the traveler continues to a different country.</p>
    <p>But:</p>
    <p><strong>United States → Shanghai → United States</strong></p>
    <p>does not meet that requirement.</p>
    <p>These examples only illustrate the routing rule. Your passport, entry port, onward travel and other conditions must also qualify. Route eligibility alone does not guarantee admission.</p>
    <p>Rather than repeating all the immigration rules here, use our:</p>
    <p><strong><a href="/china-240-hour-visa-free-transit/">China 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Guide</a></strong></p>
    <p>Or check your specific itinerary:</p>
    <p><strong><a href="/china-240-hour-visa-free-transit/#checker">Check your 240-hour transit eligibility →</a></strong></p>
    <p>U.S. passport holders are currently among the nationalities eligible for the 240-hour policy. For other China entry options, see <a href="/do-us-citizens-need-a-visa-for-china/">Do U.S. Citizens Need a Visa for China?</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="registration">How to Register for bauma CHINA 2026</h2>
    <p>The organizer offers free admission for visitors who register online in advance.</p>
    <p>To prepare for your visit:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the official bauma CHINA visitor registration page.</li>
      <li>Complete the registration form with your personal information.</li>
      <li>Follow the organizer's instructions for obtaining your admission ticket or badge.</li>
      <li>Save your registration confirmation.</li>
      <li>Bring your passport or other identification required for entry.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong><a href="https://bauma-china.com/en/trade-fair/tickets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Register for bauma CHINA 2026</a></strong></p>
    <h3>Is bauma CHINA free to attend?</h3>
    <p>Yes, for visitors who preregister online.</p>
    <p>If you register on arrival at either exhibition center, the official admission price is <strong>RMB 80 / USD 12</strong>.</p>
    <p>Your visitor ticket is valid across both venues during their scheduled exhibition days. You do not need separate tickets for SNIEC and SWEECC.</p>
    <h3>Can children attend?</h3>
    <p>Yes. According to the organizer, children may attend with a parent or legal guardian. A <strong>Minor Entry Acknowledgement Form</strong> must be completed at the onsite registration counter for free entry.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="invitation-letters">Do You Need an Invitation Letter?</h2>
    <p>If you need a visa for your visit, bauma CHINA offers an invitation-letter service.</p>
    <p>Visit the organizer's official visa information page and follow its invitation request process. The linked invitation form requires information in English and lists a submission deadline of November 2, 2026.</p>
    <p><strong><a href="https://bauma-china.com/en/trade-fair/plan-your-visit/visa-service/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bauma CHINA Visa &amp; Invitation Letter Information</a></strong></p>
    <p>For invitation-related questions, the organizer lists this contact:</p>
    <p><strong><a href="mailto:shine.gao@mm-sh.com">shine.gao@mm-sh.com</a></strong></p>
    <p>An invitation letter may support your visa application, but it does not guarantee approval. Confirm the document requirements with the Chinese embassy, consulate or visa center handling your application.</p>
    <p>If you qualify for visa-free entry, you may not need a visa invitation letter for immigration purposes.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="between-venues">How to Travel Between the Two Venues</h2>
    <p>The exhibition centers are approximately <strong>8 km apart</strong>.</p>
    <h3>Option 1: Free Shuttle Bus</h3>
    <p>The organizer plans to operate complimentary shuttle buses between SNIEC and SWEECC during the event.</p>
    <p>This is likely the easiest option if you want to visit both venues on the same day.</p>
    <p>The organizer describes frequent service throughout the day but has not published exact pickup points, first or last departures, or a timetable on its transportation page. Check again closer to your visit, especially for November 23 and 27 when only one venue is open.</p>
    <h3>Option 2: Shanghai Metro</h3>
    <p>You can also travel between the venues using Metro Lines 7 and 8.</p>
    <p><strong>From SNIEC to SWEECC:</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li>Board Line 7 at Huamu Road.</li>
      <li>Transfer to Line 8 at Yaohua Road.</li>
      <li>Get off at China Art Museum.</li>
      <li>Use Exit 3 and walk to SWEECC's North Gate.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>From SWEECC to SNIEC:</strong></p>
    <p>Take Line 8 from China Art Museum, transfer to Line 7 at Yaohua Road, and get off at Huamu Road. Use Exit 1 or 2 and walk about one minute to SNIEC Entrance 2.</p>
    <p>The organizer estimates the Metro trip at approximately <strong>40 minutes</strong>, costing RMB 4.</p>
    <p><strong><a href="https://bauma-china.com/en/trade-fair/plan-your-visit/travel-journey/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official bauma CHINA Transportation Guide</a></strong></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="airports">Flying Into Shanghai for bauma CHINA</h2>
    <p>Shanghai has two major commercial airports:</p>
    <p><strong>Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG)</strong></p>
    <p>Pudong Airport may be a convenient choice for visitors arriving on international flights, particularly those staying near the exhibition venues in Pudong.</p>
    <p><strong>Shanghai Hongqiao International Airport (SHA)</strong></p>
    <p>Hongqiao Airport is another option, especially for some regional and domestic connections.</p>
    <p>Both airports are connected to Shanghai's public transportation system. Before booking, compare your arrival airport with your hotel's location and the venue you plan to visit.</p>
    <h3>Getting to SNIEC by Metro</h3>
    <p>SNIEC is served by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Line 7:</strong> Huamu Road Station, approximately one minute's walk from Entrance 2.</li>
      <li><strong>Line 2:</strong> Longyang Road Station, approximately ten minutes' walk from Entrance 1.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Getting to SWEECC by Metro</h3>
    <p>SWEECC is served by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Line 8:</strong> China Art Museum Station, approximately five minutes' walk from the North Gate.</li>
      <li><strong>Line 7:</strong> Yaohua Road Station.</li>
      <li><strong>Line 13:</strong> Shibo Avenue Station.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Check your exhibition hall before selecting an entrance.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="hotels">Where Should You Stay in Shanghai?</h2>
    <p>Your hotel choice should depend on which exhibition venue you will visit most often.</p>
    <h3>Near SNIEC / Longyang Road</h3>
    <p>A practical choice if most of your meetings are at SNIEC. Staying near Metro Line 7 or Longyang Road can make daily travel easier.</p>
    <h3>Near SWEECC / China Art Museum</h3>
    <p>Consider this area if you are mainly attending conferences or exhibitors at SWEECC, particularly on November 23.</p>
    <h3>Visiting Both Venues</h3>
    <p>If you plan to spend time at both, choose a hotel with convenient access to Metro Lines 7 or 8, rather than assuming the two venues are within walking distance.</p>
    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> When searching for hotels, use the complete venue name. Shanghai has several exhibition centers, and booking near the wrong one could mean an inconvenient daily commute.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="practical-tips">Practical Tips for International Visitors</h2>
    <p><strong>Check your exhibitor locations first.</strong> The 2026 show spans two venues and two different date ranges.</p>
    <p><strong>Register online before traveling.</strong> Preregistration saves the RMB 80 onsite admission fee.</p>
    <p><strong>Plan extra time if visiting both venues.</strong> The two sites are separate exhibition centers, not adjoining halls.</p>
    <p><strong>Prepare mobile connectivity.</strong> The organizer says public Wi-Fi is limited at SNIEC. SWEECC offers venue-wide Wi-Fi coverage. The organizer says a Chinese mobile number is required to receive the Wi-Fi SMS verification code.</p>
    <p><strong>Save important addresses.</strong> Keep the venue name and address on your phone for taxis, navigation apps and hotel staff.</p>
    <p><strong>Check visa requirements early.</strong> If you need a Chinese visa, allow time to obtain any necessary invitation documents and complete your application.</p>
  </section>

